---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const org = "https://github.com/ternmesh";
const spec = `${org}/spec/blob/main`;
const sim = `${org}/sim`;

// draft/forwarding.md in ternmesh/spec, its Rationale: messages that arrived within a minute, and
// how many did for each second on the air, all nodes together, over three seeds.
const rows = [
    { name: "Meshtastic", fast: ["19.0%", "0.013"], own: ["1.1%", "0.001"] },
    { name: "MeshCore", fast: ["30.3%", "0.026"], own: ["5.7%", "0.004"] },
    { name: "Tern, 255 neighbours kept", fast: ["96.6%", "0.47"], own: ["22.0%", "0.019"] },
    { name: "Tern, 64 neighbours kept", fast: ["95.2%", "0.34"], own: ["20.2%", "0.017"] },
];
---

<Base
    title="Why Tern"
    description="What Tern does differently from the LoRa meshes there are, and what the simulator measured of it, with how to run it again."
>
    <section class="why wrap">
        <h1>Why Tern</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            A LoRa channel is slow and shared: every second one node spends sending is a second nobody near it can.
            Tern is designed around that, and judged by one number: how many messages arrive in time for each
            second spent on the air.
        </p>
        <p>
            Meshtastic and MeshCore are working networks, with many users and a hundred boards or so between them.
            Tern runs on one board today. This page says what it does differently, and what that was measured to
            be worth.
        </p>

        <h2 id="measured">What was measured</h2>
        <p>
            Tern's firmware's routing, run in the <a href={sim}>simulator</a> beside Meshtastic's and MeshCore's as
            the simulator ports them: 1000 nodes of which 200 relay, a message from each node every half hour to
            another at random, and a minute to arrive in. For each, the share of messages that arrived in time,
            and how many did for each second on the air, all nodes together, over three seeds:
        </p>
        <div class="table">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col"></th>
                        <th scope="col">All on SF7, 125 kHz</th>
                        <th scope="col">Each on its own preset</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {
                        rows.map((r) => (
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="row">{r.name}</th>
                                <td>
                                    {r.fast[0]}, {r.fast[1]}
                                </td>
                                <td>
                                    {r.own[0]}, {r.own[1]}
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        ))
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            In a town of 200 nodes at SF9, every node a relay, Tern delivered 100.0% in time with 255 neighbours
            kept and 99.9% with 64, and Meshtastic 21.0%.
        </p>
        <p>
            "All on SF7" puts every protocol on one fast radio setting, so that only the routing and how each one
            takes its turn on the air differ. "Its own preset" puts each on the one it ships with: Meshtastic on
            LongFast, MeshCore on the UK/EU narrow preset, and Tern on the same as MeshCore. The 200 relays are
            Meshtastic's routers, MeshCore's repeaters and Tern's relays, the rest clients.
        </p>

        <h3>What these numbers are not</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>They are from a simulator, not a deployment.</strong> Its radio model is calibrated on bench
                measurements of SX1262 radios, but its capture and isolation figures are from the literature, not a
                bench. Every number is provisional.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The incumbents are the simulator's ports of them.</strong> Meshtastic's managed flood and its
                timing are written from Meshtasticator's port of the firmware, and reproduce Meshtasticator's results
                on its own maps. MeshCore's routing is ported from MeshCore 1.17.1 and checked against MeshBench running
                MeshCore itself. A port can be wrong; the code is open for anyone to check.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Only messages to one node were sent.</strong> There is no broadcast in these runs, since
                Tern's specification has none yet, and Meshtastic and MeshCore are built around it.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The runs are in <a href={`${spec}/draft/forwarding.md#rationale`}>the draft that came of them</a>, with
            the rest of what was measured. To run them again, build the simulator beside a checkout of
            <a href={`${org}/firmware`}>ternmesh/firmware</a>, as its <a href={`${sim}#building`}>README</a> says,
            then:
        </p>
        <pre><code>tools/density.py --tsim build/tsim --fast --candidates 1,2,core
tools/density.py --tsim build/tsim --deployed --candidates 1,2,core</code></pre>

        <h2 id="design">What it does differently</h2>
        <h3>Addresses are public keys</h3>
        <p>
            A node's address is its Ed25519 public key. Adding someone is adding their key, from their node's QR
            code or link, and both of you can compare its twelve-digit short code to check it. Who you are writing to is
            something you can check, not something you are told.
        </p>

        <h3>No public channel</h3>
        <p>
            A <a href={`${spec}/draft/groups.md`}>group</a> is the people who were invited to it. A channel every
            node holds the key to is the one thing in a mesh whose cost nobody bounds: it grows with how much people
            say. Messages between two people go over a session between their nodes, which costs only the two of them.
        </p>
        <p>
            Meeting people nearby without one is drafted as <a href={`${spec}/draft/cards.md`}>presence cards</a>: a
            signed card with an address and a name, which a node sends only if its user turns it on, two hops and
            every two hours. It is a strawman, open for review, and not in the apps yet.
        </p>

        <h3>Airtime is budgeted</h3>
        <p>
            A route is measured in time on air, and a frame meant for every node is held to an
            <a href={`${spec}/draft/flooding.md#the-allowance`}>allowance</a> at each relay, which bounds what any one sender
            costs. A busy channel is meant to degrade fairly instead of collapsing. The rest of the
            <a href="/#ideas">design goals</a> are on the front page.
        </p>

        <h3>Open, and nobody's to sell</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>The specification is CC-BY-4.0, the test vectors CC0, the code Apache-2.0.</li>
            <li>Contributions are signed off under the DCO. There is no CLA.</li>
            <li>The specification will never need a fee, membership or certification to implement.</li>
            <li>
                An implementation is Tern-compatible if it passes the published conformance tests; nobody's
                permission is needed.
            </li>
            <li>
                How the project is run, and who decides what, is in
                <a href={`${spec}/GOVERNANCE.md`}>GOVERNANCE.md</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="next">
            <a class="button" href="/#start">Get started</a>
        </p>
    </section>
</Base>

<style>
    .why {
        padding-top: 32px;
        padding-bottom: 48px;
        max-width: 46rem;
    }
    h1 {
        font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
        margin: 0 0 12px;
    }
    h2 {
        margin-top: 2.5rem;
    }
    h3 {
        margin: 1.75rem 0 0.25rem;
        font-size: 1.1rem;
    }
    .lede {
        font-size: 1.1rem;
    }
    .table {
        overflow-x: auto;
    }
    table {
        border-collapse: collapse;
        width: 100%;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }
    th,
    td {
        text-align: left;
        padding: 8px 12px 8px 0;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
        vertical-align: top;
    }
    thead th {
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        color: var(--muted);
    }
    tbody th {
        font-weight: 600;
    }
    pre {
        overflow-x: auto;
        background: var(--surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        padding: 12px 16px;
    }
    .next {
        margin-top: 2.5rem;
    }
    a.button {
        display: inline-block;
        padding: 9px 16px;
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        border: 1px solid var(--accent);
        background: var(--accent);
        color: var(--bg);
        font-weight: 600;
        text-decoration: none;
    }
</style>
